course: biology
lesson: 378
title: China's mountains: golden snub-nosed monkeys, takins, red-crowned cranes and giant salamanders
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: High in the snowy mountain forests of central China, a troop of monkeys with golden fur, blue faces and almost no nose huddles together in the snow. These are golden snub-nosed monkeys, and in winter they live on lichen, bark and buds.
explain: The same mountains of Sichuan and Shaanxi hold giant pandas, takins that look like a moose, a cow and a goat mixed together, and many pheasants. In the cold fast streams lives the biggest amphibian in the world, the Chinese giant salamander.
explain: In the wetlands the red-crowned crane dances in the snow with its partner. Its red cap is bare skin, and in China and Japan it stands for long life and luck.
explain: Many of these animals are rare. China has set up reserves and green corridors of forest so animals can move between them, because forests cut into small islands leave animals hungry, with only close relatives left to mate with.
fact: Golden snub-nosed monkeys live in cold mountain forests in central China, often above 2,000 metres; in winter they eat lichen, bark and buds and huddle together, and troops can number several hundred.
fact: Scientists think the tiny nose may help against frostbite, but it is still argued; its blue face comes from the way the skin scatters light.
fact: A snub-nosed cousin in Myanmar is reported to sneeze when rain gets into its upturned nose, so it sits with its head down in rain.
fact: The takin is a big goat relative of the mountains of China and the Himalayas; its oily coat sheds rain and snow, and its big nose warms cold air.
fact: Some people think the golden takin's shining coat inspired the Greek legend of the Golden Fleece.
fact: Takins gather at places where the ground is full of salt and minerals, because their food has too little salt; over 100 can meet at one lick, and they move up and down the mountains with the seasons.
fact: The red-crowned crane's red cap is bare red skin, not feathers; only a few thousand live in the wild, in wetlands of China, Japan, Korea and Russia.
fact: Pairs of red-crowned cranes dance and call in duets and stay together for life, and young cranes dance too, maybe as practice.
fact: In Japan there is a tradition of folding 1,000 paper cranes for a wish.
fact: The Chinese giant salamander is the largest amphibian in the world, reported up to about 1.8 metres long; it lives in cold, fast mountain streams and catches fish, crabs and frogs.
fact: It can make a noise like a crying baby, so in Chinese it is called the baby fish.
fact: The giant salamander takes much of its oxygen through its skin, and the wrinkly folds along its sides give it more skin; it has four toes on each front foot and five on each back foot.
fact: Many giant salamanders were caught for food and farms bred millions; DNA tests found there are really several kinds, and farm animals released into rivers mixed them up, so scientists try to save each kind in its own river.
fact: The mountains of Sichuan and Shaanxi hold giant pandas, golden monkeys, takins and many pheasants, and China has set up reserves and green corridors so animals can move between forests.
exercise: Where do golden snub-nosed monkeys live? | In cold mountain forests of central China | In hot, wet African jungles near the equator, by big rivers | On islands in the sea | In the deserts of Australia
exercise: What do golden snub-nosed monkeys eat in winter? | Lichen, bark and buds | Bananas and ripe mangoes | Fish caught from the rivers | Bamboo shoots only, like pandas do
exercise: Why might long thick fur matter for these monkeys? | They live in snowy mountains, often above 2,000 metres | They swim in icy seas | It helps them fly | It makes them look bigger to pandas and bears that want to chase them away
exercise: Why might the monkey have such a tiny nose, some scientists think? | It may help against frostbite, but this is still argued | It was broken in fights | It helps it smell bamboo from far away across the mountains and valleys | It is a baby nose that never grew
exercise: What is a monkey's cousin in Myanmar reported to do in the rain? | Sneeze and sit with its head down | Dance with joy | Hold a big leaf over its head as an umbrella | Swim in the puddles
exercise: What is the takin? | A big goat relative of the Chinese mountains | A kind of bear | A small deer | A wild cow that came over from the plains of Africa
exercise: How does a takin's oily coat help it? | It sheds rain and snow | It makes it taste bad | It glows at night | It keeps flies away
exercise: Which legend might the golden takin's coat have inspired? | The Golden Fleece | The goose that laid golden eggs | The Sleeping Beauty | The Three Little Pigs
exercise: Why do takins lick rocks at muddy spots? | Their food has too little salt, so they lick salt and minerals | They are cleaning their teeth | They are drinking rain from the rocks after a long dry summer in the hills | They are tasting for sugar
exercise: What is the red-crowned crane's red cap made of? | Bare red skin | Bright red feathers | A red flower it wears | Red paint from people
exercise: Where do red-crowned cranes live in the wild? | Wetlands of China, Japan, Korea and Russia | Deserts of Africa | Forests of Brazil | Ice of Antarctica, along with the emperor penguins
exercise: How long do red-crowned crane pairs stay together? | For life | One spring only | Just one week | They never pair
exercise: What do people in Japan fold 1,000 paper cranes for? | A wish | A birthday cake | Fishing | A football match
exercise: What is the largest amphibian in the world? | The Chinese giant salamander | The giant cane toad of Australia | The fire salamander of European forests | The huge North American bullfrog
exercise: Why is the giant salamander called the baby fish in Chinese? | It can make a noise like a crying baby | It is always very small, even when grown up | It lives in a nursery | It hatches from fish eggs
exercise: Why does the giant salamander have wrinkly folds of skin? | More skin takes in more oxygen | To store food for winter | To hide its babies | To scare herons away
exercise: Why does it matter which river a giant salamander came from? | DNA showed there are several kinds, each from its own rivers | Every river salamander is exactly the same, so it does not matter at all | River names are written on them | Only one river has clean water
exercise: Why would a green corridor of trees help a monkey troop? | It lets them move between forests for food and new mates | It keeps the monkeys in one place | It brings them into cities, where people can feed them every day | It stops them from climbing
test: Count them: a giant salamander has 4 toes on each front foot and 5 on each back foot. How many toes altogether? | 18 | 20 | 16 | 10
test: Count them: a crane pair dances in the snow on one leg each. How many legs are in the air? | 2 | 4 | 1 | 0
test: Count them: a herd of 100 takins at a salt lick. How many horns, 2 each? | 200 | 100 | 400 | 50
test: In Kung Fu Panda, Master Crane is a fighter who flies the others about. What is right about a real red-crowned crane? | It dances and calls in duets with its life partner | It does kung fu to catch fish | It carries other animals on its back when they need to fly far away | It lives alone in a temple
test: «that monkey's got a blue face, someone painted it» What is right? | Its blue face comes from the way its skin scatters light | It paints itself with blue berries every morning to look nice for the troop | It is cold, so it turned blue | It is ill with a blue fever
test: «a salamander longer than me?? no way, they're tiny lizards» What is right? | The Chinese giant salamander is an amphibian reported up to about 1.8 metres | All salamanders are tiny lizards | It is a fish, not a salamander | It is a made-up animal
test: What would happen if a farm released salamanders from one river into a different river? | Different kinds would mix up and the local kind could be lost | Both rivers would get cleaner | Nothing, because they are all the same kind of salamander anyway, whatever river | The salamanders would swim home
test: «why would a takin need such a big nose, lol it looks dumb» What is the reason? | It warms the cold mountain air before it reaches the lungs | It is for smelling bamboo far away | It stores water for dry days | It helps it swim
test: What would happen to a monkey troop if its forest were cut into small islands? | It would go hungry, with no way to reach other forests or new mates | It would grow bigger and stronger | It would move into houses | Nothing, monkeys need little space and can live happily in a few trees for ever
test: Why would an artist paint a crane to wish someone a long life? | In China and Japan the crane stands for long life and luck | Cranes live for 1,000 years | Cranes eat the medicine plants | Cranes paint themselves
test: Why would a huge amphibian need cold water full of oxygen? | It takes much of its oxygen through its skin from the water | It cannot see well in warm water | It must stay frozen | Warm water is too salty for it
exam: Identify the principal function of the folds of skin along the flanks of the Chinese giant salamander. | They enlarge the surface through which oxygen is absorbed | They store fat for hibernation | They conceal its eggs | They serve to frighten predators by making the animal appear larger than it is
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the crown of the red-crowned crane. | It consists of bare red skin rather than feathers | It consists of red feathers | It is acquired by the bird from berries | It is present solely in juveniles
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the diet of golden snub-nosed monkeys in winter? | Lichen, bark and buds | Bananas and other tropical fruit | Fish and crustaceans of the rivers | Bamboo exclusively, in the manner of pandas
exam: Ascertain the reason for which takins assemble at certain muddy sites. | The ground there is rich in salt and minerals deficient in their food | The mud protects them from wolves | The sites are warmer than the surrounding forest during the winter months | They assemble there to give birth
